<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Healthy Minds for Healthy Bodies – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Checking in on Health</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Healthy Minds for Healthy Bodies</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze opinion editorials on general interest topics. Reading: Evaluate informational content from various sources.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze opinion editorials on health and wellness topics, identifying the writer's stance and supporting arguments (e.g., "The author believes that regular exercise improves mental health because it reduces stress and boosts energy.")
            Can evaluate informational content from articles about using healthy technology to develop good habits, identifying key points and potential biases (e.g., "The article promotes wearable fitness trackers as essential for improving exercise routines but overlooks privacy concerns related to data collection.")
            Can interpret data from health-related infographics, such as fitness plans or mental health statistics (e.g., "This chart shows that 70% of students experience stress during exam season, suggesting the need for better mental health resources.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays an infographic (visual aid) titled "Boost Your Brain: Simple Steps for Mental Well-being" showing images of exercise, healthy diet, adequate sleep, and mindfulness activities. Students work in pairs to brainstorm what "Healthy Minds for Healthy Bodies" means to them, using vocabulary from Lesson 1 like 'well-being', 'mindfulness', 'stress', 'hydration', 'energy'. Teacher asks: "What are some current trends in maintaining a healthy lifestyle that you've noticed?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading a short opinion statement about the role of technology in 'self-care', highlighting how to identify the main opinion. Teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of target sounds, contrasting /b/ in "balance" and "boost" with /p/ in "practice" and "prevent". Also, contrasts /s/ in "stress" and "sleep" with /z/ in "exercise." Teacher models the sentence rhythm when using the present perfect continuous, e.g., "I have been *practicing* meditation to *manage* my stress," emphasizing content words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is an opinion editorial? Is it factual information only, or does it include someone's viewpoint?" (Expected answer: "It presents someone's viewpoint/stance on a topic.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When you read an opinion piece, what are you looking for besides the main idea?" (Expected answer: "Supporting arguments, reasons why the author believes what they do.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Checking in on Health".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a printed or digital copy of an opinion editorial titled: "Smart Devices for Better `Health`: A Blessing or a Burden?" (approximately 300-350 words, B1.3 level). The article discusses wearable `trackers` and `apps` for `monitoring` `sleep patterns`, `activity levels`, `hydration`, and `stress`, using target vocabulary such as `well-being`, `habits`, `routine`, `energy`, `self-care`, `essential`, `preventive`, and incorporating grammar like present perfect continuous (e.g., "People have been `tracking` their `activity level`s for years.") and modals for possibility (e.g., "Wearable devices `might` `boost` your `motivation`.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Analyze and evaluate informational content from various sources, identifying key points and potential biases.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main question or debate the author is addressing in this editorial?" (Students write a one-sentence answer.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read a series of 5 statements related to the article and mark them as True or False. For false statements, they must correct the information based on the text. (e.g., "The article suggests that `trackers` are always `essential` for improving `sleep patterns`." "The author believes that excessive `monitoring` could increase `stress`.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific information and detail, identifying main arguments, evaluating evidence, and understanding the use of target grammar structures and vocabulary in context. Focus on grammar: present perfect continuous (ongoing efforts), second conditional (hypothetical situations for well-being), and modals for possibility (suggestions). Vocabulary: `health, fitness, well-being, hydration, mindfulness, balance, habits, stress, trends, tracker, app, sleep pattern, activity level, exercise, drink, sleep, practice, recommend, maintain, improve, track, manage, should, might, could, essential, preventive, consistent, regular, reliable`.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Vocabulary & Grammar Identification):</b> Students scan the article to find and underline specific target vocabulary words (e.g., `well-being`, `mindfulness`, `tracker`, `stress`, `essential`, `reliable`). Then, they identify sentences containing the present perfect continuous (e.g., "Many individuals `have been using` fitness `app`s to `monitor` their progress."), second conditional (e.g., "If I `had` a `tracker`, I `would improve` my `sleep pattern`."), and modals for possibility (e.g., "You `might` need to take a break from constant `monitoring`."). They circle these grammatical structures.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Argument Analysis):</b> In small groups, students discuss and list the author's main arguments both for and against the use of smart devices for `health` and `well-being`. They must provide direct quotes or paraphrased evidence from the text for each argument, focusing on how the author `recommends` or warns using `modals for possibility`. For instance, "The author suggests that `trackers` `could` provide valuable `feedback` on `activity level`s."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Contextualized Discussion):</b> Students work in pairs to discuss hypothetical scenarios based on the article's themes. Partner A poses a question using a second conditional (e.g., "If someone `were` trying to `manage` their `stress` using an `app`, what `preventive` advice `could` the author `recommend`?"). Partner B responds, referring to the article's content and using appropriate `modal_verbs` for `giving health suggestions`. They also practice expressing `empathy` where appropriate (e.g., "That sounds tough. The article `might` suggest trying `mindfulness` exercises.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Checking in on Health".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Article Evaluation and Mini-Presentation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task:</b> In small groups (3-4 students), students receive a second, shorter (approx. 150 words) health article or an infographic titled "The Power of `Consistent` `Hydration` and `Nutritious` `Diet` on Mental `Energy`." Their task is to:
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Read the text/infographic carefully.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify the author's main `stance` and at least two supporting `arguments`.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">`Evaluate` the `informational content` for `potential biases` or the `reliability` of the claims (e.g., "Does the article present a `balanced` view, or does it only promote one type of `diet`?").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prepare a 2-minute oral summary of their findings to present to the class. They should use `formal expressions` (e.g., "According to the article...") and maintain a `polite and respectful tone` when discussing the content. They can also briefly mention if they `would recommend` the source.</li>
            </ol>
            Students are encouraged to use the `present perfect continuous` to describe `ongoing health efforts` mentioned, `second conditional` for `hypothetical situations`, and `modals for possibility` for `suggestions` or `recommendations` found in the texts or their own `evaluation`.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and mini-presentations, using a simple checklist for identifying key reading skills and language use. Peer assessment during presentations where groups provide brief feedback to another group on clarity and arguments.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students deliver their 2-minute group summaries, demonstrating their ability to analyze and evaluate `informational content` from the second text/infographic.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify the main `stance` and `supporting arguments` (Reading comprehension).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">`Evaluation` of content for `bias` or `reliability` (Critical reading skill).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target `vocabulary` (e.g., `well-being`, `nutrition`, `hydration`, `energy`, `reliable`, `balanced`, `consistent`).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempted use of `present perfect continuous`, `second conditional`, and `modals for possibility`.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the presentation.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of `formal expressions` and maintenance of a `polite and respectful tone`.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts individually:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new insights did you gain about evaluating `health information` from reading different sources today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you now in analyzing an author's `stance` and `arguments` in an opinion editorial, especially those offering `health suggestions`?"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did you face when identifying `biased` information or unsubstantiated claims in the `health` texts, and what strategy `could` you use to overcome them next time?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can reading opinion editorials on `health` help you make more informed decisions about your own `well-being` and `lifestyle`?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully identified the main `stance` and `supporting arguments` in the texts. The `evaluation` of `potential biases` was more challenging for some, requiring further scaffolding. The use of `present perfect continuous` was natural in context, but the `second conditional` needs more explicit practice in a communicative setting. I need to ensure more varied opportunities for `giving health suggestions` and `expressing empathy` through interactive tasks. The pronunciation work on /b/ vs /p/ and /s/ vs /z/ was generally well-received. I will integrate more visual aids to support understanding of complex `health trends` in future lessons.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), we will apply the knowledge and vocabulary from today's readings to actively discuss and debate various `health strategies` and `lifestyles`. We will focus on `giving health suggestions` and `expressing empathy` during conversations, using the grammar we practiced today to talk about `hypothetical situations` and `recommendations` for improving `well-being`."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>